  "name": "Rite of First Flight",  
  "aka": [],  
  "tag": "custom",  
  "facts": [  
    {  
      "category": "general",  
      "text": "Every fledgling of Velikoye must undergo the Rite of First Flight from the bell tower before they are considered to have come of age.",  
      "since": null,  
      "held_by": null  
    },  
    {  
      "category": "general",  
      "text": "The ceremony takes place on the first day of May, when the frost has fully left the Khorol River and the nights grow short.",  
      "since": null,  
      "held_by": null  
    },  
    {  
      "category": "general",  
      "text": "During the rite, the fledgling stands at the open window of the bell tower and must spread their wings for the first time, letting the wind carry them out over the city walls.",  
      "since": null,  
      "held_by": null  
    },  
    {  
      "category": "general",  
      "text": "The bell tower of Velikoye is the tallest structure in the capital, built into the northern curtain wall above the Khorol's eastern bank.",  
      "since": null,  
      "held_by": null  
    },  
    {  
      "category": "general",  
      "text": "If a fledgling refuses or fails to fly, they are not banished but must wait until the following May to attempt the rite again.",  
      "since": null,  
      "held_by": null  
    },  
    {  
      "category": "general",  
      "text": "The bell of the tower is rung once for each fledgling who completes the rite successfully, and the sound echoes across the river valley.",  
      "since": null,  
      "held_by": null  
    },  
    {  
      "category": "general",  
      "text": "Parents and kin gather in the courtyard below the tower to watch, and it is considered a great honor to have a fledgling who flies cleanly across the walls without faltering.",  
      "since": null,  
      "held_by": null  
    },  
    {  
      "category": "general",  
      "text": "The rite draws its name from the moment when the young owl-folk must leave the safety of the nest — in this case, the bell tower — and navigate the open sky on their own wings.",  
      "since": null,  
      "held_by": null  
    },  
    {  
      "category": "general",  
      "text": "In the event of high winds or foul weather, the rite is postponed, as flying in dangerous conditions would endanger the fledgling's life.",  
      "since": null,  
      "held_by": null  
    },  
    {  
      "category": "general",  
      "text": "The tradition dates back to the founding of Velikoye as the capital of the Tsardom of Moscavia, when the first Tsar declared that no owl-folk should live within his walls who had not proven themselves against the sky.",  
      "since": 871,  
      "held_by": null  
    },  
    {  
      "category": "general",  
      "text": "The scars of the Siege of Velikoye on the city walls serve as a reminder of why the rite's flight must carry the fledgling beyond those walls — to symbolize the courage needed to defend the realm.",  
      "since": null,  
      "held_by": null  
    }  
  ],  
