  "name": "Corners of the Earth",
  "aka": [],
  "tag": "doctrine",
  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The world is divided into four great regions, or Corners, each defined by its cardinal position relative to a theoretical center.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Northern Corner encompasses all lands north of the 45th parallel, including the frozen wastes, the boreal forests, and the high mountain ranges.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Southern Corner stretches from the 45th parallel southward to the equatorial belt, encompassing warm grasslands, southern seas, and volcanic archipelagos.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Eastern Corner covers the vast lands to the east of the Prime Meridian, including steppe kingdoms, eastern coastlines, and the great silk trade routes.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Western Corner includes all territories to the west of the Prime Meridian, from the Atlantic coastlands to the interior river valleys and temperate forests.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The dividing lines between Corners are not rigid borders but broad transitional zones where cartographers acknowledge overlap and cultural blending.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Each Corner is understood to have its own prevailing climate, dominant flora, and characteristic peoples, though no Corner is homogeneous.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The doctrine holds that no Corner is superior to another, but each possesses strengths and vulnerabilities that shape the balance of the world.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Cartographers at the Academy of Northern Lights in Velikoye have refined the boundaries of the Corners over centuries of surveying and debate.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The concept of the Corners predates the Academy by at least two centuries, emerging from early navigators and astronomers who sought to organize the known world.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Some scholars argue for a fifth Corner encompassing the polar regions, but the four-Corner model remains the standard taught at the Academy.",
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
