  "name": "Corners of the Earth",
  "aka": [],
  "tag": "cartographic doctrine",
  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The world is divided into four great regions, each called a Corner, marking the cardinal extremities of the known continents.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Northern Corner encompasses the frozen reaches, permafrost steppes, and auroral skies of the far north, where the Academy of Northern Lights stands as its preeminent seat of learning.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Southern Corner stretches across sun-scorched deserts, ancient jungle canopies, and volcanic archipelagos, home to peoples whose histories predate any northern chronicle.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Western Corner holds vast grasslands, temperate forests, and coastline kingdoms built on maritime trade, where no single power has yet achieved lasting dominion.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Eastern Corner is a land of towering mountain ranges, silk roads, and imperial courts whose bureaucracies rival the complexity of nature itself.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Each Corner possesses its own cartographic traditions, often in disagreement with the others over the precise boundaries between them.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The doctrine holds that to understand the world, one must study each Corner on its own terms, for no single Corner contains the full measure of human civilization.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The dividing lines between Corners are not fixed but shift as new lands are discovered and old borders are redrawn by war or treaty.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Cartographers trained in the Northern Corner believe the aurora borealis marks the spiritual heart of the Northern Corner, and that its patterns encode geographical truths.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Academy of Northern Lights has formalized the Corners of the Earth into a standardized teaching curriculum since at least the year 872.",
      "since": 872,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Not all cultures acknowledge the four-corner division; some nomadic peoples consider the world a single unbroken plain, and island civilizations sometimes add a fifth Corner for the ocean.",
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
